            <ArticleTitle>Chronic daily headache with migrainous features due to papilledema-negative idiopathic intracranial hypertension.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>451-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Patients with the syndrome of chronic daily headache often report migrainous symptoms and consequently are diagnosed as having a primary headache syndrome. We report two cases of idiopathic intracranial hypertension causing chronic daily headache with migrainous features in the absence of associated papilledema.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
